  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe serves 4 and takes about 30 minutes."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tonkatsu"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is one of Japan's most popular Western-influenced dishes. The"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "panko"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "coating fries to a light, shatteringly crisp shell. It is served with shredded cabbage and a drizzle of"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "tonkatsu sauce"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pound the cutlets to an even thickness. This is the most important preparation step. Thin, even cutlets cook through quickly and stay juicy. Thick or uneven cutlets overcook on the outside before the centre is done."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 4,
          "items": [
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "pork-shoulder",
              "amount": 600,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "cut into 4 steaks, about 1.5cm thick",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "panko",
              "amount": 120,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "cornflour",
              "amount": 50,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "eggs",
              "amount": 2,
              "unit": null,
              "prepNote": "beaten",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "vegetable-oil",
              "amount": 500,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": "for frying",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "tonkatsu-sauce",
              "amount": 2,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": "to serve",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "japanese-mayonnaise",
              "amount": 2,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": "to serve, optional",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place each pork steak between two sheets of cling film. Pound with a meat mallet or the base of a heavy saucepan until about 1cm thick and even throughout."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season the pork lightly with salt on both sides. Set up a breading station: cornflour in one shallow dish, beaten egg in another, and panko breadcrumbs in a third."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Coat each pork steak in cornflour and shake off the excess. Dip in beaten egg, letting any drips autumn back into the dish. Press firmly into the panko on both sides. Set on a plate."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the vegetable oil into a deep frying pan to a depth of about 2cm. Heat to 170°C. Use a kitchen thermometer to check the temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fry 2 cutlets at a time for 3 to 4 minutes on the first side until golden brown. Turn carefully and fry for 3 more minutes on the second side. The cutlet is done when the coating is deep golden and a thermometer reads 65°C at the thickest point."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain on a wire rack for 2 minutes. Do not rest on kitchen paper or the base will steam and soften. Slice each cutlet into 2cm strips."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve on a plate with finely shredded cabbage. Drizzle tonkatsu sauce over the cutlet. Add a small pot of Japanese mayonnaise on the side if liked."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Coating is greasy and not crispy",
              "cause": "The oil temperature was too low when the cutlets went in.",
              "fix": "Make sure the oil reaches 170°C before frying. Let the oil come back up to temperature between batches. Too-cool oil makes the panko absorb fat instead of crisping."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Coating is dark brown but pork is still raw inside",
              "cause": "The oil was too hot, browning the outside too quickly.",
              "fix": "Fry at a steady 170°C and use the thermometer. At 170°C a 1cm cutlet should be cooked through by the time the coating is golden, around 6 to 7 minutes total."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Panko coating is patchy and falls away when sliced",
              "cause": "The cornflour layer was too thin or the cutlet was wet when breaded.",
              "fix": "Pat the pork dry with kitchen paper before breading. Coat the cornflour layer firmly. The egg needs a dry base to stick to. Press the panko on hard from both sides."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tonkatsu arrived in Japan in the late 19th century as part of Western-influenced yōshoku cooking. It quickly became a staple and now appears in lunchboxes, rice bowls, sandwiches, and curry. The thick panko crust sets it apart from European schnitzel. Rest the cutlet after frying so the crust stays crisp."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}